#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135186 :tutorial/content "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable.\n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X$, the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x)$, then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration.\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n$$\n{(2)/(π(1 + x^{2})), \\mid x ∈ [0, ∞); 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(x)= 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x < 0$, we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0$.\n\nTo compute the CDF for\n\n$$\nx\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= \\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{0}f(t)dt + \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= 0 + \\int_{0}^{x}(2)/(π(1 + t^{2}))dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{2}{π}\\arctan t\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x - \\frac{2}{π}(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "de1bbdf6-687d-537a-a6fe-03d9fafbe841" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135187 :tutorial/content "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nonly, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a$, and\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b$.\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < a \\\\ \\int_{a}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b; 1, \\mid x > b\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0794419f-6b72-568b-a787-d71121c8bcd9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8365 :tutorial/title "Finding a CDF Given a PDF With Lower and Upper Bounds"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135188 :tutorial/content "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq x \\leq 1)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le x \\le 1) &= F(1) - F(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-1}} - \\frac{1}{1 + e^{0}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-1}} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.231\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e5203710-822b-55d4-9dfb-985bc4d41d6a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8366 :tutorial/title "Using a CDF to Compute a Probability Over an Interval"}]}
